# RUN_MODE controls everything: LIVE does real research, MOCK exercises the
# orchestration offline with canned data and no keys.
RUN_MODE=LIVE

# --- LLM -------------------------------------------------------------
# Any OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint. Embeddings are generated locally, so
# the provider only needs to serve chat completions.
#
# TWO models, not one, because a run makes two kinds of call:
#
#   LLM_MODEL       ~30 calls  judgement — query planning, adjudicating a
#                              claim, the narrative, /ask
#   LLM_BULK_MODEL  ~120 calls mechanical — turning a page into claim JSON,
#                              and Graphiti's entity extraction
#
# The bulk calls fill a fixed schema from text in front of them. There is no
# judgement in them, and Small 4 costs a third of Large 3 ($0.15/$0.60 per 1M
# tokens against $0.50/$1.50). Pointing both settings at the same model is
# the single most expensive change you can make here.
#
# Mistral (default). Get a key at https://console.mistral.ai — phone
# verification, no card. Its free "Experiment" plan is the only free tier
# that can finish a city: 1B tokens/month against the 400-600k a run needs,
# so you are throttled on request RATE and never walled on volume. Check
# yours in Admin Panel > API > Limits; it decides how long a run takes, not
# whether it finishes.
LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.mistral.ai/v1
LLM_MODEL=mistral-small-latest
LLM_BULK_MODEL=mistral-small-latest
LLM_API_KEY=
#
# Medium rather than Large for judgement because Large 3 is NOT served on the
# free tier — it is absent from GET /v1/models for a free key and returns
# 403 'tier_not_allowed' (code 1910). On a PAID key switch LLM_MODEL to
# mistral-large-latest: it is both stronger and ~3x cheaper than Medium 3.5
# ($0.50/$1.50 per 1M against $1.50/$7.50), so this default is the expensive
# one once you start paying.
#
# Confirm what your own key can reach rather than trusting either of us:
#   curl https://api.mistral.ai/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer $LLM_API_KEY"
#
# Pin the generation (mistral-large-3, mistral-small-4) or the exact build
# (mistral-large-2512, mistral-small-2603) if you need reproducibility: the
# -latest aliases roll across generations, and Mistral's own lifecycle policy
# warns that behaviour AND pricing can change under them.
#
# Two Mistral notes worth knowing before you demo:
#   - Requests on the free Experiment plan may be used to train Mistral's
#     models unless you opt out in the Admin Console (Privacy). Paid plans
#     are opted out by default.
#   - Mistral's Batch API is 50% cheaper AND exempt from rate limits, which
#     would remove the throttle entirely. It needs a submit-and-poll
#     rewrite of app/llm/client.py, so it is not wired up here.
#
# Alternatives, both settings each:
# DeepSeek    LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1 · no free tier, but no
#             RPM/TPM/RPD limits either — only 2500 concurrency, which is the
#             best shape available for this burst workload. ~$0.13/city.
#             Set LLM_BULK_REASONING_EFFORT= and pass thinking disabled, or
#             it silently reasons at "high" on every call.
# Gemini      LLM_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/
#             Models gemini-3.5-flash / gemini-3.1-flash-lite, efforts
#             low / minimal. Free tier is 20 req/DAY and cannot finish one
#             pass; a billed Tier 1 key works and costs ~$0.45/city.
# Groq        LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1 · rejects
#             reasoning_effort, so clear BOTH settings. Free tier caps at
#             100k tokens/day on the 70B model — about a fifth of one city.
# OpenAI      LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# Ollama      LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1 · unmetered and keyless.
#             The only option with no rate limit at all. Full recipe below.

# --- Running inference locally with Ollama ----------------------------
#
# The one configuration with NO rate limit and NO key, which is the reason to
# reach for it: a city costs 150-250 calls, and every hosted free tier meters
# that somewhere. Here the only budget is your own wall-clock.
#
#   1. Install Ollama from https://ollama.com/download, then pull a model:
#
#        ollama pull qwen3:8b
#
#   2. Start the server with a context window this pipeline can use. THIS
#      STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL and is the one everybody skips:
#
#        Windows PowerShell:  $env:OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768; ollama serve
#        macOS / Linux:       OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768 ollama serve
#
#      Ollama defaults to a small window and TRUNCATES longer prompts rather
#      than refusing them. Graphiti's entity-extraction prompts are past 4k,
#      so the default produces a run that completes, writes a graph with no
#      fact edges, and reports no error anywhere. The OpenAI-compatible API
#      has no way to set this per request, so it has to be set on the server.
#      `python -m scripts.check_services` checks it and tells you if it is
#      too small.
#
#   3. Uncomment the four lines below, leave LLM_API_KEY empty, and comment
#      out the LLM_REASONING_EFFORT line further down — it is set after this
#      block, so it wins, and "high" on a local model buys minutes of
#      deliberation rather than a better brief.
#
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_MODEL=qwen3:8b
# LLM_BULK_MODEL=qwen3:8b
# LLM_BULK_REASONING_EFFORT=none
#
# On the model: 8B is what fits a normal laptop and it does run the whole
# pipeline, but extraction quality is below hosted Small 4 and the brief will
# be thinner. If you have the memory, qwen3:30b or gpt-oss:20b are closer.
# Below ~7B, structured extraction starts inventing fields and the trust
# machinery has less to work with.
#
# On speed: unmetered is not the same as fast. 150-250 calls against a local
# 8B is tens of minutes on a GPU and hours on CPU. The job endpoints exist
# precisely so this is a progress bar rather than a timeout.
#
# On reasoning effort: both settings default to EMPTY whenever LLM_BASE_URL
# is local, because a thinking model asked to deliberate locally spends
# wall-clock instead of money, and LM Studio and llama.cpp do not all
# implement the field. Ollama does implement it, which is why setting
# LLM_BULK_REASONING_EFFORT=none above is worth it on a hybrid model like
# qwen3 — it explicitly turns thinking off for the ~120 bulk calls.
#
# This is a LOCAL path only. A Railway free-tier container has 0.5 GB of RAM
# and cannot host a model; deployments keep using a hosted provider.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which model Graphiti runs its own extraction on. Defaults to LLM_BULK_MODEL,
# which is where the majority of a run's calls live.
# GRAPHITI_MODEL=

# Thinking effort — a cost control, not a quality dial: thinking tokens bill
# at the OUTPUT rate and come out of the same budget as the answer.
#
# With both profiles on Small 4, these two values ARE the judgement/bulk
# split. Valid values on Small 4 are only "none" and "high". "none" is a value
# Mistral defines (answer directly, no thinking trace), not an absence, and it
# is where most of the token saving on a run comes from.
#
# Empty means "send no such field", which is what a model that does not
# implement the parameter needs: a shim rejects an unknown parameter rather
# than ignoring it, so an effort aimed at the wrong model 400s every call.
# If you switch LLM_MODEL to mistral-medium-latest or -large-latest, blank
# LLM_REASONING_EFFORT in the same edit — neither implements it.
#
# Both default to empty when LLM_BASE_URL points at localhost. The values
# below are written out, so they apply to a local server too — comment them
# out if you switch to Ollama and want the local defaults.
LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=high
LLM_BULK_REASONING_EFFORT=none

# Output ceilings. These bound thinking tokens too, so cutting them to the
# size of the expected answer backfires: a model that runs out mid-thought
# returns an empty string, which every caller here reads as "nothing found".
LLM_MAX_TOKENS=4096
LLM_BULK_MAX_TOKENS=2048

# Graphiti's separate, larger budget for entity extraction. It has no
# reasoning_effort setting, so on a thinking model headroom is the only
# protection against empty responses. Set too low, the symptom is a knowledge
# graph with no fact edges rather than an error.
GRAPHITI_MAX_TOKENS=16384

# --- Embeddings (local, no API key) ----------------------------------
# Shared by Milvus and by Graphiti's embedder and reranker.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=all-MiniLM-L6-v2

# --- Search discovery -------------------------------------------------
# Leave blank to fall back to duckduckgo-search (no key required, lower quality).
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# --- Relational store -------------------------------------------------
# Point at Postgres in a real deployment:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg2://user:pass@host:5432/cardio4cities
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./data/cardio4cities.db

# --- Vector store -----------------------------------------------------
# A path ending in .db runs Milvus Lite embedded — no server to operate.
# For a real cluster: http://localhost:19530, or a Zilliz Cloud endpoint.
MILVUS_URI=./data/milvus.db
MILVUS_TOKEN=
MILVUS_COLLECTION=city_passages

# --- Graph store: Neo4j Graph Database Sandbox ------------------------
# Create at https://sandbox.neo4j.com, open the "Connection details" tab and
# copy the Bolt URL + password. A Sandbox looks like bolt://<ip>:7687; an Aura
# instance looks like neo4j+s://<id>.databases.neo4j.io — either works.
# Sandboxes expire after 3 days (extendable to 10) and come back with a NEW ip
# and password, so re-copy both rather than only the password.
NEO4J_URI=bolt://0.0.0.0:7687
NEO4J_USER=neo4j
NEO4J_PASSWORD=

# --- Research policy --------------------------------------------------
# How many independent sources a fact needs before it can be called VERIFIED.
MIN_SOURCES_FOR_VERIFIED=2
# How many of the five dimensions must yield a usable fact before the brief is
# written. Below this, the planner re-plans the dimensions that came back empty.
MIN_DIMENSIONS_COVERED=3
MAX_PLANNER_RETRIES=2

# --- Cost and latency budget ------------------------------------------
# Each of these bounds a multiplier. Raise them for a more thorough (slower,
# pricier) run; lower them for a faster demo.
QUERIES_PER_DIMENSION=2
MAX_SOURCES_PER_DIMENSION=4
MAX_CLAIMS_PER_PASSAGE=3
FACT_CHECK_CONTEXT_PASSAGES=4

# --- Source domain policy ---------------------------------------------
# Applied in the search agent, BEFORE the per-dimension cap above, so a source
# the policy was never going to allow cannot first consume one of the few
# slots its dimension gets. Both are comma-separated; matching is on a dot
# boundary, so `gov.in` covers `nhm.maharashtra.gov.in` and not `evilgov.in`.
#
# The denylist is an editorial rule, not a performance one: a user-generated
# video or a social post is not citable evidence in a public-health brief no
# matter how relevant a search engine finds it. Rejections are logged rather
# than warned - excluding a recipe blog is the policy working, not the run
# degrading. Uncomment to override the built-in list.
# SOURCE_DOMAIN_DENYLIST=youtube.com,youtu.be,facebook.com,instagram.com,twitter.com,x.com,tiktok.com,pinterest.com,reddit.com,quora.com,linkedin.com,tripadvisor.com,amazon.com
#
# The allowlist is EMPTY by default, and that is a deliberate choice rather
# than an oversight. Restricting discovery to domains someone already trusted
# makes a demo fast and repeatable, but it guarantees the brief can only
# contain what was expected of it - and the useful finding is usually the
# source nobody nominated. When it is set the run DISCLOSES the restriction in
# the report, because a gap from a narrowed search would otherwise read as an
# absence of published information.
# SOURCE_DOMAIN_ALLOWLIST=gov.in,nic.in,who.int,worldbank.org
FACT_CHECK_CONTEXT_CHARS=1500
# How many claims share one adjudication call. Lower to 1 to restore strictly
# one call per claim, which is the most conservative setting.
FACT_CHECK_BATCH_SIZE=5

# How much of a page is KEPT (indexed for /ask) versus how much is SENT to
# the claim extractor. The second is the run's largest single payload — one
# call per source, carrying the page — and the window sent is chosen by
# overlap with the dimension brief rather than taken from the top of the
# page, so the smaller budget loses boilerplate rather than signal.
PASSAGE_CHAR_LIMIT=5000
EXTRACTION_CHAR_LIMIT=2500

# --- Concurrency ------------------------------------------------------
# Parallel LLM calls during extraction and fact-checking. Keep at or under
# your provider's request-rate allowance. 1 is right for a throttled free
# key; raise to 4-8 on a paid key for roughly that much more speed.
LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY=1
# Parallel work that costs no tokens: robots.txt, the X-Robots-Tag HEAD
# request, page fetches. Separate from the above because it is limited by
# sockets rather than by a quota — sharing one number meant the provider's
# rate limit silently throttled the crawler too.
HTTP_MAX_CONCURRENCY=8
# Parallel search queries. Deliberately lower: `ddgs` scrapes consumer
# engines and answers a burst with a Ratelimit error.
SEARCH_MAX_CONCURRENCY=3
# Parallel Graphiti episode writes. 1 is not timidity — add_episode resolves
# each entity against the existing graph, so concurrent episodes naming the
# same organization can both create it. Raise it to shorten the run's longest
# node, accepting some duplicate entities.
GRAPH_WRITE_CONCURRENCY=1
# Graphiti's OWN concurrency, which LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY does not govern. It
# defaults to 20 internally and is the largest caller in a run, so this is
# the setting people miss. Raise it together with LLM_MAX_CONCURRENCY above.
GRAPHITI_SEMAPHORE_LIMIT=1
HTTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=12

# --- Research jobs ----------------------------------------------------
# POST /research registers a job and returns an id to poll; it does not wait
# for the run. Concurrency is capped because SQLite takes one writer at a
# time — raise it only with DATABASE_URL pointed at Postgres.
MAX_CONCURRENT_RESEARCH=2
# How long a finished job stays pollable. The brief itself is stored
# permanently, so expiry costs the progress detail and not the deliverable.
JOB_RETENTION_SECONDS=3600
# Load the embedding model at startup rather than on the first request, so the
# ~60s cold-start load does not land on a user. Worth it for a demo, but it
# allocates ~1 GB during startup: on a memory-capped host the container is
# OOM-killed before it can log why, which looks like an application crash and
# is not one. Off by default. Check the host's memory ceiling, then enable.
PREWARM_EMBEDDINGS=false

# --- Observability (OpenTelemetry) ------------------------------------
#
# Off by default. When off there is no provider, no exporter and no export
# thread, and every span call site yields one shared no-op object - which is
# what keeps this compatible with a 0.5 GB container. When on with the
# packages missing, it logs once and continues without telemetry; it never
# stops a research run.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE. A city is 150-250 model calls across ten nodes and three
# datastores, and when the brief comes back thin the question is always
# "which part gave up". The pipeline already measured itself - per-node
# timings, per-model tokens - but only after the run, only in this process,
# and only if you went looking. A trace is the same measurement, openable.
#
# WHAT YOU GET. One trace per run, rooted at `research <city>`, with the ten
# nodes under it, every model call under the node that made it (GenAI
# semantic conventions, with token counts), every embedding batch, the Milvus
# and Neo4j round trips, outbound page fetches, and SQLAlchemy queries.
# Prompts and completions are deliberately NOT recorded: they carry scraped
# page content, which does not belong in a trace backend.
#
# OTEL_ENABLED=true

# Simplest possible start - spans to stdout, no collector to run:
# OTEL_ENABLED=true
# OTEL_CONSOLE_EXPORT=true

# Or ship to anything speaking OTLP/HTTP. Jaeger all-in-one is one command:
#   docker run --rm -p 16686:16686 -p 4318:4318 jaegertracing/all-in-one
#   then open http://localhost:16686
# OTEL_ENABLED=true
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS is read by the SDK itself and works as usual,
# which is how you authenticate to Honeycomb, Grafana Cloud or Datadog.

# --- An LLM-native dashboard: Opik or Langfuse ---
# Both ingest OTLP/HTTP natively, so either is configuration only. Both want
# HTTP and not gRPC, which is the exporter already pinned in requirements.txt.
#
# OPIK (Comet). Self-hosting is free: `./opik.sh`, UI on localhost:5173.
# OTEL_ENABLED=true
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://www.comet.com/opik/api/v1/private/otel
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=<api-key>,projectName=cardio4cities,Comet-Workspace=<workspace>
# ...or self-hosted, where no headers are needed:
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:5173/api/v1/private/otel
#
# LANGFUSE. Basic auth built from the two project keys:
#   AUTH=$(printf '%s' 'pk-lf-...:sk-lf-...' | base64 -w0)
# OTEL_ENABLED=true
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://cloud.langfuse.com/api/public/otel      # EU
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://us.cloud.langfuse.com/api/public/otel   # US
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:3000/api/public/otel           # self-hosted
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=Basic <AUTH>,x-langfuse-ingestion-version=4
#
# Langfuse reads the gen_ai.* attributes this app already emits and renders
# those spans as generations with model, token and cost figures - the semantic
# conventions are what make the dashboard understand the trace.
#
# Both take traces and NOT metrics, so metrics turn themselves off when the
# endpoint is one of theirs rather than erroring every 60s against a URL that
# cannot accept them. OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_ENDPOINT overrides that.

# Record prompts and completions on LLM spans. OFF by default because they
# contain scraped third-party page content, and a trace backend is a copy of
# it that nobody audits.
#
# You almost certainly want this ON for Opik, Langfuse or LangSmith, where the
# prompt/completion pair is the entire product - with it off those dashboards
# draw a correct trace tree and then show an empty panel on every span, which
# reads as a broken integration rather than a deliberate default. It is a real
# decision though: turning it on ships page content to a third party.
# OTEL_CAPTURE_CONTENT=true
# OTEL_CAPTURE_CONTENT_CHARS=4000

# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=cardio4cities
# OTEL_SERVICE_VERSION=0.1.0
# Metrics alongside traces: token counters, node-duration histogram, facts by
# confidence tier. Traces answer "what happened in this run"; these answer
# "what is it costing" over time. Ignored unless OTEL_ENABLED is on.
# OTEL_METRICS_ENABLED=true
